rion requires a fitted resistive DC path connecting /IN to /OUT, with no conduction assumed through unpopulated footprints and no alternate assembly override. In the saved design the only element between net /IN and net /OUT is R1 (10k, R_0805), and R1 is marked do-not-populate in both the schematic and the board.\",\"mechanism\":\"With R1 unpopulated, independent connectivity analysis of the saved board shows three disjoint DC islands, each net-pure: /IN (J1 pad 1, track ae12bf6f, R1 pad 1), /OUT (J1 pad 2, R1 pad 2, C1 pad 1, tracks 8b528360/a6dab0b4/69a2edc1), and GND. The only span between the /IN and /OUT islands is the absent R1 body across a 0.8 mm copper gap between R1 pad inner edges (x=104.6 to x=105.4 at y=100). J1 pin 1 to J1 pin 2 is therefore an open circuit as assembled; the fitted C1 is DC-open and provides no path. The copper routing is otherwise complete (bridging R1's pads joins the islands), so the failure is purely the population state.\",\"severity\":\"blocking\",\"assessment\":\"defect\",\"evidence\":[{\"path\":\"inputs/project/rc_filter.kicad_sch\",\"locator\":\"line 1261: (dnp yes) on symbol R1 (uuid c5c38e1a-7593-5c99-b971-2f9d2e7bef78)\"},{\"path\":\"inputs/project/rc_filter.kicad_pcb\",\"locator\":\"line 304: (attr smd dnp) on footprint R1 (uuid 8404a180-a151-464e-8f25-8cf316cdcba5); R1 pads at (104.0875,100) net /IN and (105.9125,100) net /OUT\"},{\"path\":\"work/netlist/connectivity.json\",\"locator\":\"components.R1.dnp = true; nets./IN = [J1.1, R1.1]; nets./OUT = [C1.1, J1.2, R1.2]\"},{\"path\":\"work/geometry/geometry.json\",\"locator\":\"footprints[1] ref R1 dnp=true; tracks list shows /IN copper ends at R1 pad 1 (104.0875,100) with no other /IN-to-/OUT conductor\"},{\"path\":\"review-work/physical/experiments/fitted_connectivity.json\",\"locator\":\"fitted_path_IN_to_OUT = false; islands list; r1_pad_gap_mm = 0.8\"},{\"path\":\"review-work/physical/experiments/notes.md\",\"locator\":\"section 'Independent fitted-assembly DC connectivity check'\"},{\"path\":\"inputs/requirements.txt\",\"locator\":\"line 1: fitted resistive DC path must connect /IN to /OUT; do not assume an unpopulated footprint conducts\"}],\"reasoning\":\"The requirement is explicit and the native files are unambiguous: both the schematic symbol and the board footprint for R1 carry the DNP attribute, and no alternate assembly override exists. My pcbnew-based analysis modeled only fitted conductors (fitted resistors bridge their pads; fitted capacitors are DC-open; DNP footprints conduct nothing) and corrected for a mid-segment track T-junction at (107.5,100) so the /OUT copper is fully merged; even so, /IN remains isolated. Zero-violation ERC/DRC exits do not contradict this because KiCad connectivity treats DNP pads as net members; a successful tool exit is not proof of electrical behavior. This directly fails the sole evaluated criterion, hence blocking.\"},{\"id\":\"PHY-2\",\"title\":\"Verification coverage gap: all packet checks pass while the sole functional criterion fails, because no check is assembly-population-aware\",\"location\":\"packet checks erc/drc/netlist/geometry (work/erc/erc.json, work/drc/drc.json, work/netlist/connectivity.json, work/geometry/geometry.json)\",\"condition\":\"The packet's four checks all report pass/info with zero violations on a board whose fitted assembly fails the stated /IN-to-/OUT requirement. ERC has no rule that a DNP part breaks a required path; DRC connectivity and schematic parity treat the DNP footprint's pads as valid net members (and dnp is set consistently in both files, so parity sees no mismatch); netlist and geometry merely record dnp as information without evaluating the fitted path.\",\"mechanism\":\"A reviewer or gate relying on these green results would conclude the design is sound. The open circuit between J1 pin 1 and pin 2 in the saved assembly is only revealed by a population-aware connectivity analysis, which no packet check performs.\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"as